After manganese, carbon is the principal alloying element in medium-manganese steels. The carbon content in these alloys exhibits a wide range, from ultra-low concentrations of approximately 0.01 wt.% to high levels up to 1 wt.% [ 84 ]. However, for applications pertinent to the automotive industry—specifically the production of flat products for car body manufacturing, which constitute the focus of this article—the carbon content is generally maintained below 0.3 wt.% [ 53 ]. In medium-Mn steels, carbon plays a pivotal role in governing the mechanical and thermal stability of retained austenite at room temperature following heat treatment. This stability, in turn, critically affects the kinetics and mechanisms of austenite transformation during mechanical loading through the transformation-induced plasticity and twinning-induced plasticity effects, as well as the potential manifestation of the Portevin–Le Chatelier (PLC) phenomenon, i.e., dynamic strain aging [ 55 ]. Analogous to manganese, carbon effectively stabilizes austenite to room temperature by lowering both the  γ \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
